# Go SQL Query Formatting

When writing SQL in Go (for `db.Query`, `db.QueryRow`, `db.Exec`, and similar), format SQL for balanced readability: avoid very long lines and avoid unnecessary vertical expansion for tiny queries.

## Rules

- Use raw string literals (backticks) for SQL queries.
- Keep SQL line length around 100 chars (hard limit 120). If a line exceeds this, split it.
- Short/simple queries may stay compact (for example single-clause `DELETE ... WHERE id = ?`).
- Split major clauses onto separate lines when queries are medium/long (`SELECT`, `FROM`, `WHERE`, `ORDER BY`, `LIMIT`, `OFFSET`, `INSERT`, `VALUES`, `UPDATE`, `SET`, `DELETE`).
- For long `SELECT` lists (or when the `SELECT` line is too long), put one column per line.
- For long `INSERT` column lists, put one column per line inside parentheses.
- For long `UPDATE ... SET` statements, put one assignment per line.
- Prefer this balance:
  - tiny query: compact is fine
  - medium query: clause-per-line
  - long query: clause-per-line + one item per line in long lists

## Examples

```go
// Bad
db.QueryRow(`SELECT id, name, created_at FROM users WHERE id = ?`, id)

// Good (long query -> expanded)
db.QueryRow(
	`SELECT
		id,
		name,
		created_at
	FROM users
	WHERE id = ?`,
	id,
)

// Good (short query -> compact)
db.Exec(`DELETE FROM users WHERE id = ?`, id)
```

